{"number": 102, "text": "       48             Makingthingseasier\n\n\n                      Choosing    your   approach\n\n\n                                          When  you need to work out the easiest way to do something, you have to explore\n                                          different ways of approaching the task. Sometimes there will be many feasible’\n                                          alternatives to consider and sometimes there will only be one or two viable? options.\n                                          You need to enlist the help of people to give you advice before you decide on which |\n                                           method to adopt.\n                                           The best advice is to take a step-by-step approach: break the task down into stages, _\n                                           so that you are dealing with small, manageable chunks. This way you willsoon find\n                                           that something you thought was hard is in fact simplicity itself*. But be careful, don’t\n                                           always take what looks like the easy option. Think first. At the end, you can feel proud\n                                           that you didn’t just take the easy way out*\n\n                      1 possible       3 extremely simple\n                      2 workable       4 do what is easiest but not what is best - which may mean avoiding doing anything at all\n\n                      Ways   of  remembering\n                      Matica tha callacatiance in thic tavt intradicina       far           vaur\n\n                         WAYS      OF   REMEMBERING                          :                                         |\n\n                                   A trained memory  is an immense asset’, particularly in public life. Mnemonics are\n                                   systems we use to help us remember complex  information easily, and people who use\n                                   them can recall things with an amazing degree of accuracy.\n                                              The Ancient Greeks perfected” a number of mnemonic  techniques, some\n                                              of which are still used today, for example, using the first letter of each item\n                                              to form a word that will help you remember the whole list of items. For\n                                              instance, you might remember the female first name ADA to help you recall\n                                              the phrase amazing degree of accuracy. Or you might think of the word BAG,\n                                              which will give you three colours that collocate with hair (blond, auburn,\n                                              ginger).\n                                            rule of all memory training systems is the idea of ‘Initial Awareness’. This\n                                   works in a straightforward? way. The basic principle is that the system helps you\n                                   concentrate the mind  on whatever you are trying to remember for just long enough to\n                                   force Initial Awareness.\n                                   All memory training systems are perfectly simple. Once you have taken the time to\n                                   learn them, you will be able to instantly recall any new items of information you want\n                                   to, with remarkable ease. You may find it a steep learning curve’, but | assure you that\n                                   it is worth the effort.\n\n                       1 something of great value            3 easy to understand or simple\n                      2 the verb is pronounced /p3:'fekt/    4 something which is difficult to learn and takes a lot of effort\n\n\n                         Do you know  any mnemonic  techniques to help you learn          If possible, share\n                         them  with other students.\n\n\n                       English Collocations in Use Advanced\n        100", "char_count": 4116, "mean_conf": 91.37, "word_count": 464, "words": [{"text": "100", "x0": 153, "y0": 3354, "x1": 214, "y1": 3381, "conf": 96, "bold": true}, {"text": "When", "x0": 838, "y0": 660, "x1": 933, "y1": 690, "conf": 96, "bold": false}, {"text": "you", "x0": 945, "y0": 668, "x1": 1006, "y1": 697, "conf": 96, "bold": false}, {"text": "need", "x0": 1020, "y0": 659, "x1": 1102, "y1": 689, "conf": 96, "bold": false}, {"text": "to", "x0": 1113, "y0": 663, "x1": 1148, "y1": 689, "conf": 96, "bold": false}, {"text": "work", "x0": 1159, "y0": 659, "x1": 1244, "y1": 688, "conf": 96, "bold": false}, {"text": "out", "x0": 1255, "y0": 661, "x1": 1312, "y1": 688, "conf": 97, "bold": false}, {"text": "the", "x0": 1322, "y0": 657, "x1": 1378, "y1": 688, "conf": 96, "bold": false}, {"text": "easiest", "x0": 1389, "y0": 661, "x1": 1506, "y1": 687, "conf": 96, "bold": false}, {"text": "way", "x0": 1517, "y0": 665, "x1": 1584, "y1": 695, "conf": 96, "bold": false}, {"text": "to", "x0": 1595, "y0": 660, "x1": 1629, "y1": 687, "conf": 96, "bold": false}, {"text": "do", "x0": 1641, "y0": 656, "x1": 1683, "y1": 686, "conf": 96, "bold": false}, {"text": "something,", "x0": 1694, "y0": 656, "x1": 1886, "y1": 693, "conf": 96, "bold": false}, {"text": "you", "x0": 1897, "y0": 663, "x1": 1958, "y1": 692, "conf": 96, "bold": false}, {"text": "have", "x0": 1972, "y0": 655, "x1": 2051, "y1": 684, "conf": 97, "bold": false}, {"text": "to", "x0": 2062, "y0": 658, "x1": 2095, "y1": 683, "conf": 96, "bold": false}, {"text": "explore", "x0": 2107, "y0": 653, "x1": 2243, "y1": 690, "conf": 96, "bold": true}, {"text": "different", "x0": 839, "y0": 705, "x1": 997, "y1": 735, "conf": 96, "bold": true}, {"text": "ways", "x0": 1008, "y0": 712, "x1": 1099, "y1": 742, "conf": 96, "bold": true}, {"text": "o